# Tobii Eye Tracker 5 (EyeChip) — access without root
#
# # Why the file is called 60- and not 99-
#
# `uaccess` is the mechanism that grants access: systemd-logind puts an ACL on
# the device for whoever is logged in at the seat, and removes it when they log
# out. But the tag alone does nothing — what acts on it is
# /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/73-seat-late.rules:
#
#     TAG=="uaccess", ENV{MAJOR}!="", RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"
#
# udev processes rule files in lexical order, so a file named 99- sets the tag
# twenty-six files AFTER the only rule that reads it. This one used to be
# 99-tobii.rules, and the consequence was measurable on a machine where it was
# installed and the tracker plugged in:
#
#     $ udevadm info -q all -n /dev/bus/usb/007/002 | grep TAGS
#     E: CURRENT_TAGS=:uaccess:
#     $ getfacl -p /dev/bus/usb/007/002
#     user::rw-   group::rw-   other::rw-      <- no ACL for the logged-in user
#
# Tagged, and no ACL: the builtin had already run. So `uaccess` was decorative
# and MODE="0666" was doing all the work — which is why the mode could not
# simply be tightened without moving the file first.
#
# # Why MODE is 0660
#
# 0666 hands every local process — every sandboxed application, every other
# user on the machine — access to an infrared camera pointed at the person
# using it. With the tag now effective, the ACL is the way in and the mode does
# not have to be.
#
# On a system without logind there is no ACL and the device stays root-only.
# That is a deliberate trade: defaulting the other way makes every desktop less
# safe in order to serve the few systems that are not one. See the README
# ("If the tracker still needs root") for the group-based alternative.

# Bootloader / DFU mode
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="2104", ATTR{idProduct}=="0102", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
# Runtime mode
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="2104", ATTR{idProduct}=="0313", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Virtual joystick (optional — safe to delete the rule at the end of this file)
#
# The "Virtual joystick" output presents head pose and gaze as a game
# controller, which is how a native Linux or Proton game receives head tracking
# without Wine and without opentrack. Creating one needs write access to
# /dev/uinput, which is root-only by default on most distributions.
#
# What this grants: any program running as the logged-in user may create
# virtual input devices — that is, synthesise keystrokes and pointer motion
# into the session. That is a real capability, and it is the same one Steam,
# KDE Connect and Logitech's own rules already grant on machines that have
# them; this file is written the same way they are. Delete the last line below
# if you do not want it. Everything except the virtual joystick still works,
# and the hub will say so rather than failing silently.
#
# static_node= creates the node before the module is loaded, so the first
# program to open it triggers the autoload rather than finding nothing there.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"
